  <div class="kathi-border thin"></div>
  <div class="bio-body">
    <div>
      <h3>Where I Come From</h3>
      <p>I grew up in <strong>Kathiawad</strong> — the Saurashtra peninsula of Gujarat, a place of extraordinary built heritage. Kathiawadi towns have a distinct urban logic: the <em>chowk</em> at the centre, the <em>pol</em> neighbourhood system, the jharokhas that project from every facade. Long before I knew the word "urbanism," I was already living inside one of its most sophisticated experiments.</p>
      <p>That upbringing gave me an intuition that cities are not neutral containers for people, but active participants in shaping behaviour, identity, and power. The placement of a well, the width of a lane, the temple at a crossroads: everything is a decision, and every decision is political.</p>
      <h3>What I Write About</h3>
      <p>This blog is about <strong>urban design</strong> in the broadest sense — the forces that determine why cities look and feel the way they do. Zoning law, yes, but also colonial street plans and their afterlives, the economics of gentrification, the psychology of public space, the politics of parking. I write from the perspective of someone who moves between cities rather than living exclusively in one.</p>
      <p>I'm particularly interested in the tension between <strong>heritage and modernity</strong> in South Asian cities: how Ahmedabad, Jamnagar, and Rajkot are negotiating their architectural pasts in the face of rapid economic growth.</p>
      <h3>Photography & Observation</h3>
      <p>I photograph cities the way a naturalist photographs birds — patiently, at street level, looking for the moments when the built environment reveals its logic. A broken step worn smooth by a century of feet. A wall repainted twelve times, each colour bleeding through. These images are the raw material of the essays.</p>
      <h3>A Note on Perspective</h3>
      <p>I write with a <strong>situated point of view</strong> — from the Global South, from a small city rather than a megacity, from a pedestrian rather than a planner. So much urban writing assumes the vantage point of the wealthy, Western, automobile-owning professional. That is not me, and this blog reflects it.</p>
    </div>
